otential:  \n1. W7-W8 and something.  \n2. T8 pair.  \n3. B2-B4 and something.  \n\nPassing keeps more options open. Taking Chi commits me to a specific meld but leaves bad shape and less chance for high-scoring patterns since all melds will be exposed except the last one possibly.\n\n**Evaluation:** Two promising moves: Chi W8 to progress toward a quick win but leaves difficult remaining tiles, or Pass to keep hand flexible and possibly aim for a concealed last meld for extra points.  \nGiven the difficulty of the leftover tiles after Chi, and since I already have two exposed melds, scoring will be low unless I get a special pattern \u2014 but with two exposures already, high-value patterns unlikely. Better to keep hand flexible and maybe draw into a better shape.\n\n**Reason:** Chi W8 fixes a meld but leaves W4, B2, B4, T8, T8 \u2014 hard to complete a legal winning hand (needs one more meld and a pair, but here W4,B2,B4 can't form a meld, so must break T8 pair to try to make melds, which is worse). Passing lets me draw a tile that might complete a meld with existing tiles (like B3 for B2-B4, or W6/W9 for W7-W8, or T7/T9 for T8 sequence), keeping T8 pair intact.\n\n**Answer:** Pass"}
